# CHANGELOG lines that claim a speed and that no benchmark case measures, each with the reason (scripts/check-bench-cases.sh).
# Format: a phrase of the line (it must stay in the Unreleased section), a tab, the reason. A line that a case measures needs no entry here (its row of bench/cases.tsv names it);
# an entry whose phrase matches no claim, or that has no reason, fails the check. Short on purpose: a speed that can be measured gets a case, not a line here.
`point_columns` (`sift_col` reloption, default none	an access-method lookup (sift.enable_col_scan on, a few rows returned), timed per query by bench/col_scan.py (kind point, next to the btree, classes and key_columns kinds); the cases of bench/cases time aggregate plans (SiftAgg), which a point lookup does not use
an index scan of `sift_col` that selects a few rows of a unit	an access-method lookup, timed per query by bench/col_scan.py (kinds point, classes, set); the cases of bench/cases time aggregate plans, which a point lookup does not use
the `sift.col_*_ops` classes of a `sift_col` index cover boolean	an access-method scan, timed by bench/col_scan.py (kind classes) next to the btree
a `sift_col` index built with the operator classes `sift.col_int2_ops`	a bitmap index scan, timed by bench/col_scan.py (kind classes) next to the btree
the aggregate scan counts the tuples of a chunk once	measured in user instructions per execution (2 to 7% of one query), below what the wall-clock rounds of a case resolve; the counts are in the line
the aggregate scan finds the NULL tuples of an integer	measured in user instructions and branch mispredictions per execution (+1.1% instructions), not in time
is read by walking the elements the key marks	measured in user instructions (-3.7%), below what the wall-clock rounds of a case resolve
translated the bound of each partition again	measured in instructions of the start of a scan of 4000 partitions (48 to 30 million), not in time
the table `VACUUM` honours the cost delay	a throttle: the VACUUM is slower on purpose, as an autovacuum worker is; no speed is claimed for it
the extension SQL goes from 3.2 to 3.3	an upgrade notice; the numbers in the line are version numbers
`scripts/plangate.sh`, a plan-choice gate	a test gate that times its own shapes (win, tie or lose at 1 ms) for a human to read; not a benchmark of one optimization
`test/t/pg_upgrade.sh` runs `pg_upgrade --check`	a test script; the numbers are PostgreSQL versions
records why a per-unit key presence summary	records a design that was not built; its 7.8x and 2.0x are from a prototype
leave the visibility map of the table empty	the same measurement as cluster_visibility_map (a scan priced for a heap check after CLUSTER, 320 ms against 82 ms forced)
the UNION ALL arms of a two-level or fused scan go from 4 to 16	a limit on the width of a plan shape (slug shape_width), not a speed
the group table of a `Custom Scan (SiftAgg)` is bounded by `hash_mem`	bounds peak memory (VmHWM) and spills by design, so it is slower where it spills; the case would measure memory, which the runner does not
the reloption `return_columns = 's, u'` of a `sift_col` index makes the named text	an access-method index-only scan, off by default; its own numbers are a loss in memory (0.055 against 0.155 ms), timed by bench/col_scan.py like the other access-method scans
is stored in blocks of 64 strings that a reader decompresses one at a time	the first statement of a new backend (a cold dictionary), timed per backend (median of 3 to 5 new backends) by hand and by the focused tests; the cases of bench/cases time repeated plans, in which the parsed dictionary is cached, so the speed they would show is the warm one that the line says does not change
`sift.col_auto_merge` (off, 3.2) also vacuums the table	timed by the update, delete and VACUUM section of the README (bench/steps/store_section.py, lane sift_maint) with the setting on, against the same lane with it off; not an aggregate plan, which the cases of bench/cases time
a lookup join keyed by columns of an earlier lookup's table	measured in instructions and cycles per execution (211.9M to 184.5M), the cache entries it saves are counted by the test agg_lookup_chain; not a wall-clock difference the rounds of a case resolve
planning and starting a scan of a partitioned table cost less for each partition	measured in instructions (a plan over 1,000 partitions in half, a generic EXECUTE over 4,000 in less than half); the planning time at 400 and 3,200 partitions is the cases partitions_scan and partitions_many
the choice between a shared common table expression and the inline plan added up the cost	the same shape and the same test (agg_cte_inline_rows) as the case cte_series_arms, which anchors the other line of the pair
the parallel top-N scan (workers on `ORDER BY key LIMIT n`) bounds the superblocks	counts the units a walk reads (5 against 143), not time: at these sizes the start of the workers is the 7 to 12 ms; the runner's one-CPU quota cannot show workers
the outer aggregate over per-row aggregates	the numbers are 0, 2 and 4 workers; the runner measures one backend by default (a run with --cpus 5 has workers), so it is a run of its own, not a row of the block
a `SiftAgg` path reports to the plan above the groups of every row	an estimate: the groups the plan expects against those made (2, 5, 10 against 16,667 to 66,667), not a time; covered by the plan gate (scripts/plangate.sh)
its group arrays are indexed by the keys' ids together	measured on a 195,000-row query with a calendar of days left-joined to unnested json (47.5 -> 5.5 ms); the differential test agg_group_sparse proves the result, and the bench dataset has no such shape yet
the groups of an aggregate scan go to the hash table, not to the direct arrays	memory, not time: the largest peak of a backend over 258 statement shapes, 310 to 159 MB (the time of the five shapes that change is 9% less); the differential test agg_group_wide proves the rows
no longer skips an index over `sift.vacuum_rewrite_limit`	measured by the churn simulator (bench/churn/churn.sh, the server worker arm, 1,000,000 rows, 12 rounds): units answerable and the grouped sum after each round, not a plan of a case of bench/cases
keeps the units its zones and summaries cleared for the next begin	instructions of a scan with 782 units that every predicate prunes (callgrind, the second changelog line of the zone pass); the differential tests col_zone_window and coldiff prove the rows; a timing needs a table of thousands of units, which a bench case does not build
tests the zones of the units of that morsel only	instructions of a scan with 782 units that every predicate prunes (callgrind); measured at 15,389 units on a separate host (44.9 to 5.1 ms for a sum, 88 to 8.3 ms for an avg); the differential test col_zone_window proves the rows
keeps the checked head of a segment	instructions of a hit on a table of 782 units (callgrind, uuid, integer and text columns); the differential tests col_seg_head, col_zone_window, col_damage and coldiff prove the rows and the misses; a unit of the bench dataset has no such shape yet
counting the all-visible pages of the heap	instructions a unit of a scan (callgrind, tables of 391 and 782 units); the tests aggstub_dict and agg_preagg* compare the count with the visibility map's own, and the bench dataset has no such shape yet
`point_tid_columns` (`sift_col` reloption, default none	an access-method lookup (sift.enable_col_scan on, a few rows returned), timed per query by bench/col_scan.py (kind point_tid, next to the btree and point_columns); the cases of bench/cases time aggregate plans (SiftAgg), which a point lookup does not use
finds the superblocks the zones and summaries leave a unit in once for the scan	instructions a unit of a scan that prunes nearly every unit (callgrind, 500 and 1,000 units); the differential tests col_skip_map (with and without stray rows), col_zone_window and agg_zone_or prove the rows and the units counted
ran the ordinary plan when it had bound parameters	a fresh backend's first statement, timed once per path on a tmpfs cluster (60k rows, no preload): 222 to 262 ms without the fix, 7 to 10 ms with it, for the extended protocol, EXECUTE with arguments and pgbench -M extended/prepared; a backend's first statement is not a case of bench/cases (the harness backend is warm); the test agg_first_statement proves the plan and the rows
a run of a correlated `LIMIT 1` child per distinct input is priced at half	a change of the cost model that moves plans of the 258 captured statements; they need a mapping table and the captured corpus, which the cases of bench/cases do not carry (the timings are in the pull request)
a group table that spilled starts the next table of the scan with room for as many groups	instructions of one aggregate scan of 38,646 groups under work_mem = 1MB (496 to 459 million, and the time, as the line states); a spilling scan is not a case of bench/cases; the answers do not change
`sift.table_auto_defrag_dead_pct` is 5 (was 20)	measured as the line states: the store benchmark's churn phases at 200,000 rows and a four-client mix of update, insert and delete (160,000 transactions, medians of 3); a background pass under concurrent clients is not a single query of bench/cases
`sift.table_auto_defrag` is on by default	measured as the line states: a four-client mix of update, insert and delete for 30 s, pass on against off, medians of 3, at 25,000, 200,000 and 1,000,000 rows; a background pass under concurrent clients is not a single query of bench/cases
`sift.rls_scores` (superuser setting, off by default)	measured as the line states: ranked top 10 at 100,000 documents with 50%, 5% and 0.5% of the rows visible; a role under a row-level security policy is not a case of bench/cases
merges the address lists of its segments by runs	an access-method scan that the planner takes for a key it expects to find early (a LIMIT) only when forced: the numbers are callgrind instruction counts and bench/col_scan.py timings (kind point_tid, query lim_grp10); the sort it removes is also in the plan of the case point_key_range
